    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0361" n="315" xml:id="pg315"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">945C.61/11–752: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consulate General
                        at Salisbury</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d133-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-11-14T18:07:00-05:00">November 14, 1952—6:07
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>38. Dept appreciates urtel 52<anchor n="2" xml:id="d133-f2"/> re <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Financial Times</hi> story and agrees with your proposal
                    hold text statement contained Deptel 32<anchor n="3" xml:id="d133-f3"/> for
                    future use shld situation warrant. Accordingly shld alleged US position again be
                    referred in press, public speeches, legis debates etc. you are instrd release
                    statement to local press with such minor drafting changes as appear to be
                    necessary. Dept also suggests you incorporate in text statement if released
                    point that USGovt has been closely fol Federation question since its inception
                    as matter gen internatl interest but has consistently felt this complex question
                    for determination by govts and peoples concerned.</p>
                <p>In view great effect outcome Federation issue may have on public opinion in Af as
                    well as overseas and in UN where matter recently mentioned<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d133-f4"/> Dept considers it very important USGovt’s attitude of
                    impartiality be publicized if repetition <hi rendition="#italic">Financial
                        Times</hi> incident occurs.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bruce</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d133-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d133-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was repeated to London; drafted by Feld of the Bureau of African
                    Affairs (AF); and cleared by the Office of Dependent Area Affairs (UND), the
                    Economic and Investment Development Staff (ED), and the Office of British
                    Commonwealth and Northern European Affairs (BNA).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d133-f2" n="2" xml:id="d133-f2-content">
                <p><ref target="#d132"><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d133-f3" n="3" xml:id="d133-f3-content">
                <p>Dated Nov. 4, <ref target="#pg313">p. 313</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d133-f4" n="4" xml:id="d133-f4-content">
                <p>This happened at the Oct. 24, 1952 meeting of the Trusteeship Committee. For
                    further information, see United Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Official
                        Records of the General Assembly, Seventh Session, Fourth Committee,
                        Trusteeship</hi>, p. 32.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
